# The Nodes list's filter row: which of the roster is below it, as a control the reader can see
# and a press the frame names.
#
# Four things to watch, and the last is the one that would be a bug nobody could see.
#
#   - The row is a *Settings field*, drawn by the same `struct inkcell_fb_list_item` an enum row on the
#     Settings tab is: a label naming the axis, INKCELL_ICON_EDIT in the gutter, and the set in
#     the value column. It was a chip strip that filled the row on its own and carried no mark
#     saying it could be pressed, which is what made the top of this screen read as a caption
#     about the list rather than a control over it.
#   - Left and Right are what move it, and the action bar says so with the same `<->` keycap the
#     Settings bar uses. The shoulders still walk the tabs - that split is the whole reason the
#     d-pad's axis could be spent here, and it is the map's split one screen along.
#   - The heading's count is what is *drawn*, not what is held. "Nodes (n of m)" already means
#     "there is more than this", so a filter needs no sentence of its own; watch the first number
#     fall as the row steps and the second stay where it is.
#   - Under Direct, every row has a staircase against its trailing edge and none of them says
#     "hop" or "mqtt". That is the row asking mesh_ui_node_signal_heard() rather than a looser
#     question of its own - if it asked `hops_away == 0` instead, Delta Camp would be in this
#     list with nothing beside its name.

scene demo
scale 4
clock 2026-01-12 19:12
config

# One node the reader has chosen to keep, so the last segment has something to show.
pin ECHO

tab nodes
hold 1600

# All -> Direct. The nodes heard with no relay in the way, which is the question the trailing
# staircase answers one row at a time and this answers for the whole list.
key right
hold 2200

# Direct -> Pinned. The star in the marker gutter is the same fact this segment filters on, which
# is why the segment is called what X is called.
key right
hold 2200

# And back the way we came, which is the half a strip stepped only by A never had: the axis runs
# both ways, so a reader who has gone one too far presses the other direction rather than
# wrapping the whole set.
key left
hold 2000

# The rows under it are still rows: the cursor walks off the control into them, Left and Right go
# back to being the tab switch there, and A opens a node exactly as it does with no filter on.
# This is the half that is a picture of a bug if it is wrong - a filtered row that opened the
# roster's node rather than the row's would look right.
key down 6
hold 1200
key a
hold 1800
key b
hold 700
